# OMN-13437 / OMN-13418 Phase 2a: un-forgeable prod-promotion trust anchor.
# This file is the canonical grant registry. Platform leads are the intended
# reviewers of any change to it, but this CODEOWNERS entry is ADVISORY, not
# enforced: `required_pull_request_reviews` is intentionally NOT enabled on
# `dev` (enabling it would block a platform-lead-authored grant PR from ever
# being approvable, since GitHub rejects self-approval of your own PR — see
# feedback_no_approval_required). The only MECHANICAL self-approval defense
# is `validate-prod-promotion-grants` (OMN-14441): approved_by must not equal
# the PR author. That check is a complete defense only while
# @OmniNode-ai/platform-leads has exactly one member — a live tripwire
# (check-platform-leads-review-tripwire, OMN-14445) fails CI the moment a
# second member joins and this line's claim would otherwise start silently
# lying. See OMN-14445 for the fix options being evaluated.
grants/prod_promotion_grants.yaml @OmniNode-ai/platform-leads

# OMN-16702: PUBLIC-cluster staging-class namespace authorization anchor.
# Same ADVISORY status as the prod anchor above — `required_pull_request_reviews`
# is intentionally NOT enabled on `dev`, so this line does not mechanically
# block anything. Stated plainly so it is not mistaken for enforcement: the
# mechanical defenses on THIS anchor are scope-narrowing, not approver-
# authentication. `validate-staging-namespace-grants` (ci.yml) pins scope,
# runtime_lane, cluster and the {dev, onex-dev} namespace allowlist fail-closed,
# so the worst a forged entry can reach is two staging-class namespaces on the
# public cluster. It can never reach onex-prod: the prod gate reads a different
# file and matches only `runtime_lane == "prod"`.
grants/staging_namespace_grants.yaml @OmniNode-ai/platform-leads
